<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Sacred War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 449–448 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Sacred War was a brief conflict around 449–448 BCE over control of the sanctuary of Delphi. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> sent an army that took the sanctuary from the Phocians and handed it to the Delphians, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> marched in and returned it to the Phocians. It formed part of the rivalry between Athens and Sparta before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-sacred-war.jpg" alt="Second Sacred War" width="320" height="331" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FlankerFF · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phocian_league.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 458 or 457 BCE the Phocians captured three towns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dori">Doris</a>, the region the Spartans regarded as their mother country. A Spartan army marched north, defeated the Phocians and restored Dorian rule. On its way back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> an Athenian force attacked it but was repelled, and the Spartans returned home. After the Five Years' Truce between the two powers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> set out to check what historians have called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>' imperialistic ambitions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-greece">Central Greece</a>, where the Phocians were allies of Athens.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The war turned on who should hold the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> at Delphi, seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythia">Pythia</a>. The Phocians, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, had control of the sanctuary. A Spartan expedition quickly removed them and returned its stewardship to the Delphians, restoring their self-government. Once the Spartans had gone home, an Athenian army led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> took the city and installed Phocian rule again.</p><p>The chronology is disputed. One group of historians, among them Karl Julius Beloch, Benjamin Dean Meritt, Theodore Wade-Gery and Malcolm Francis McGregor, followed the Greek historian Philochorus and placed the Spartan march in 449 BCE, with the Athenian return some time later. Another group, led by Arnold Wycombe Gomme and Felix Jacoby, rejected Philochorus' dates and placed both marches on Delphi in 448 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Scholars have found no evidence that these changes in the governance of Delphi had any effect on the pilgrims who came to consult the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythia">Pythia</a>. The episode is one of only two conflicts that writers of classical antiquity themselves called a sacred war, the other being the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-sacred-war">Third Sacred War</a> of 356–346 BCE. This struggle over Delphi also formed one episode in the wider contest between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> for influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-greece">Central Greece</a> in the years after the Five Years' Truce.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Sacred War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>Second Sacred War</b> of about 449–448 BCE Sparta handed the sanctuary to the Delphians, after which Athens returned it to the Phocians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Military</a></li></ul></section>
